{
  "dom_id": "step-q362200",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=x+6$ and $y={x}^{2}.$ Find the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=x+6$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ x+6 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=3.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14217745/assets/dd8dc332f56d3b851647e5ed0fa8208eb5d3daf6e704a42ab5cffdb78ed78585.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=x+6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{3}((x+6)^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{3}({x}^{2}+12x+36-{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+6{x}^{2}+36x-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})\\,{|}_{-2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{27}{3}+54+108-\\frac{243}{5})-(-\\frac{8}{3}+24-72+\\frac{32}{5})] \\\\  & =\\frac{500\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
